ng company it got 10 billion  dollars in relief it came under the  regulation of stephen freeman an  ex-goldman sachs employee and could  still afford to pay its employees 11  billion dollars in bonuses in the first  half of 2009.  goldman sachs didn't suffer at all  goldman sachs has had its best years  since the mortgage meltdown and since  the worldwide economic crisis they knew  their mortgages would fail they never  told anyone and what was goldman sachs  punishment for hiding the true value of  mortgages that had sold returning a  measly 14 million dollars to the  treasury less than a third of the ceo's  salary that same year and paying a few  pathetic fines in the tens of millions  which again is the cost of doing  business remember for goldman to be  winners there had to be losers  [Music]  after 2008  people were obviously reluctant to put  their money into any more convoluted  financial instruments so gomen had to  find something more real more physical  so they turned to the commodities market  when gas prices hit four dollars and  eleven cents a gallon in 2008 the  government wrote it off as having an  issue with supply but it was far from  the truth the supply of crude oil had  actually been increasing and demand  decreasing so the price of gas should  have gone down if it wasn't for goldman  sachs you see goldman and a lot of other  powerful firms had switched to trading  oil futures basically they were getting  the same pension funds and other big  investors from the housing mania to  agree to buy oil at a certain price on a  certain dates so oil futures became the  go-to tool for goman and other big banks  to speculate on and by 2008 a barrel of  oil was traded 27 times before actually  being delivered and used and with each  trade the price increased eventually  people owned more future barrel oils  than there were actual barrels of oil in  existence goldman promised people ever  increasing oil prices and when in mid  2008's crude oil prices dropped